quad c \\:|\\: b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{b}}={\\color{blue}{3}}.$ Then, $3^3\\mid 3^2\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}$ but $3^3\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{3}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{b}}={\\color{blue}{2}}.$ Then, $20\\mid 30\\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}}$ but $20\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2^5, 3^4)=1.$ Therefore, we have $2^5 \\: | \\: 3^4b$ and $\\text{gcd}(2^5, 3^4)=1,$ which implies $2^5\\: | \\: b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198203 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $3 \\: | \\: a$ and $21 \\: | \\: a$ then $63 \\: | \\: a$\n\n- If $3 \\: | \\: b$ and $22 \\: | \\: b$ then $66 \\: | \\: b$\n\n- If $2^3 \\: | \\: c$ and $3^2 \\: | \\: c$ then $72 \\: | \\: c$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198203 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $3 \\: \\mid \\: a$ and $21 \\: \\mid \\: a$ then $63 \\: \\mid \\: a$\n2. If $3 \\: \\mid \\: b$ and $22 \\: \\mid \\: b$ then $66 \\: \\mid \\: b$\n3. If $2^3 \\: \\mid \\: c$ and $3^2 \\: \\mid \\: c$ then $72 \\: \\mid \\: c$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186198203 1074 "Recall that for any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$,\n\n> $a \\: \\mid \\: c$, $\\quad b \\: \\mid \\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\: \\mid \\: c$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{21}}$. Then, $(3\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}})$ and $(21\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}})$, but $(63\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}})$.\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1$ and $3\\cdot 22 = 66$. Therefore, we have $(3 \\: \\mid \\: b), (22 \\: \\mid \\: b)$, and $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1$, which implies $(66\\: \\mid \\: b)$.\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1$ and $2^3\\cdot 3^2 = 72$. Therefore, we have $(2^3 \\: \\mid \\: c), (3^2 \\: \\mid \\: c)$, and $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1$, which implies $(72 \\: \\mid \\: c)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186198203 1074 "Recall that for any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$a \\:|\\: c,$ $\\quad b \\:|\\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\:|\\: c.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{a}}={\\color{blue}{21}}.$ Then, $(3\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}})$ and $(21\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}}),$ but $(63\\not\\mid {\\color{blue}{21}}).$\n\n- Statement II is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1$ and $3\\cdot 22 = 66.$ Therefore, we have $(3 \\: | \\: b), (22 \\: | \\: b),$ and $\\text{gcd}(3, 22)=1,$ which implies $(66\\: | \\: b).$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1$ and $2^3\\cdot 3^2 = 72.$ Therefore, we have $(2^3 \\: | \\: c), (3^2 \\: | \\: c),$ and $\\text{gcd}(2^3, 3^2)=1,$ which implies $(72 \\: | \\: c).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198207 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $5 \\mid m$ then $25 \\mid m$\n\n- If $25 \\mid m$ then $10 \\mid m$\n\n- If $25 \\mid m$ then $5 \\mid m$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198207 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $5 \\mid m$ then $25 \\mid m$\n2. If $25 \\mid m$ then $10 \\mid m$\n3. If $25 \\mid m$ then $5 \\mid m$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186198207 1074 "For any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$, we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n> $a \\mid b \\quad$ and $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{5}}$. Then, $5\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$ but $25\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$.\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{25}}$. Then, $25\\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$ but $10\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$.\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $5 \\mid 25$. Therefore, we have\n$5\\mid 25$ and $25\\mid m$, which implies that $5 \\mid m$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186198207 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$ we have transitivity of divisibility:\n\n$a \\mid b \\quad$ and $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{5}}.$ Then, $5\\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}$ but $25\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let ${\\color{blue}{m}}={\\color{blue}{25}}.$ Then, $25\\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}$ but $10\\not \\mid {\\color{blue}{25}}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $5 \\mid 25.$ Therefore, we have\n$5\\mid 25$ and $25\\mid m,$ which implies that $5 \\mid m.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420258 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420263 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420265 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420267 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198309 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420268 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420259 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420260 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420261 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198310 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420269 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420257 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420262 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420264 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198311 1038 #edb/ref 17592186420266 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198548 1050 540.0 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198890 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198890 1071 "Let $d, h$, and $f$ be integers. If $d∣f$, $h∣f$, and $gcd(d, h) = 1$ then {{field-1}}." 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186198890 1071 "Let $d,h,$ and $f$ be integers. If $d∣f,$ $h∣f,$ and $\\text{gcd}(d,h)=1$ then\n\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186198890 1074 "For any integers $d,$ $h,$ and $f,$ we have that\n\n$$\nd∣f,\\,h∣f,\\,\\text{and}\\,\\text{gcd}(d,h)=1\\,⟹\\,dh∣f.\n$$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199012 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $15\\,|\\,32b$ then $15\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,7b$ then ${7}^{2}\\,|\\,b$\n- If ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,25b$ then $9\\,|\\,b$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199012 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. If $15 \\mid 32b$ then $15 \\mid b$\n2. If $7^{2} \\mid 7b$ then $7^{2} \\mid b$\n3. If $3^{2} \\mid 25b$ then $9 \\mid b$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186199012 1074 "For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$\n\n$c\\,|\\,ab\\,$ and $\\,\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\,⟹\\,$ $\\,c\\,|\\,b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1.$ Therefore, we have $15\\,|\\,32b$ and $\\text{gcd}(15,32)=1,$ which implies $15\\,|\\,b.$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, let $b=7.$ Then, ${7}^{2}∣7\\cdot 7$ but ${7}^{2}∤7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. First, note that $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1.$ Therefore, we have ${3}^{2}∣25b$ and $\\text{gcd}({3}^{2},25)=1,$ which implies ${3}^{2}\\,|\\,b.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199581 1064 "Recall that if $a$ and $b$ are integers where\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe notation\n$a \\mid b$\nmeans that $a$ divides $b$. This means there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = a \\cdot k\n$$\n\nOn the other hand,\n$a \\not\\mid b$\ndenotes the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$.\n\nThis simple definition can be used to prove some basic properties of integer divisibility." 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186199581 1064 "Recall that if $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a \\neq 0,$ the notation \n\n$$\na \\mid b\n$$\n\nmeans that $a$ divides $b.$ This means there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = a \\cdot k.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, \n$a \\not\\mid b$\ndenotes the fact that $a$ does not divide $b.$\n\nThis simple definition can be used to prove some basic properties of integer divisibility." 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199582 1064 "Let's now state our next property, known as the **divisibility of a product:**\n\n**Theorem**\n\n> *For any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$,*\n> $\\qquad$ $c \\mid ab \\quad$ *and* $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $c \\mid b$\n\nThis statement assumes that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1\n$$\n\n(i.e., that $a$ and $c$ are coprime). This suggests we may want to use *Bézout’s identity* to prove this statement since it relates two integers to their greatest common divisor.\n\nRecall that Bézout’s identity states that for any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's prove this statement.\n\n**Proof**\n\n> *Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$, by Bézout’s identity there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n> $au + cv = 1$.\n> *On the other hand, since $c\\: \\mid \\: ab$, there exists an integer $k$ such that*\n> $ab=ck$.\n> *Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $b$ and substituting $ab=ck$, we get the following:*\n> $au + cv|= 1; (au + cv)b|= b; (ab)u + cvb|= b; (ck)u + cvb|= b; c(ku + vb)|= b$\n> *As $k,u,v$, and $b$ are integers, then $ku+ vb = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n> $cm = b$\n> *which implies that*\n> $c\\: \\mid \\: b$." 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186199582 1064 "Let's now state our next property, known as the **divisibility of a product:** \n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ $c \\mid ab \\quad$ *and* $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $c \\mid b$\n\nThis statement assumes that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ (i.e., that $a$ and $c$ are coprime). This suggests we may want to use *Bézout’s identity* to prove this statement since it relates two integers to their greatest common divisor. \n\nRecall that Bézout’s identity states that for any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's prove this statement.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1,$ by Bézout’s identity there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + cv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $c\\: | \\: ab,$ there exists an integer $k$ such that*\n\n$$\nab=ck.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $b$ and substituting $ab=ck,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+cv & =1 \\\\ (au+cv)b & =b \\\\ (ab)u+cvb & =b \\\\ (ck)u+cvb & =b \\\\ c(ku+vb) & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,u,v,$ and $b$ are integers, then $ku+ vb = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\ncm = b\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nc\\: | \\: b.\n$$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199583 1064 "Let's now state our final property, known as the **divisibility of an integer by a product:**\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ *$a \\:|\\: c,$ $\\quad b \\:|\\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\:|\\: c.$*\n\nThe proof is given below.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + bv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $a\\: | \\: c$ and $b\\: | \\: c,$ there exist integers $k$ and $l$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=ak,\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nc=bl.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $c$ and substituting $c=ak$ and $c=bl,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+bv & =1 \\\\ (au+bv)c & =c \\\\ auc+bvc & =c \\\\ au(bl)+bv(ak) & =c \\\\ ab(ul+vk) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,l,u,$ and $v$ are integers, then $ul + vk = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\nab\\cdot m = c\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nab\\: | \\: c.\n$$" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199583 1064 "Let's now state our final property, known as the **divisibility of an integer by a product:**\n\n**Theorem**\n\n> *For any integers $a$, $b$, and $c$,*\n> $\\qquad$ *$a \\: \\mid \\: c$, $\\quad b \\: \\mid \\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\: \\mid \\: c$.*\n\nThe proof is given below.\n\n**Proof**\n\n> *Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n> $au + bv = 1$.\n> *On the other hand, since $a\\: \\mid \\: c$ and $b\\: \\mid \\: c$, there exist integers $k$ and $l$ such that*\n> $c=ak, \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad c=bl$.\n> *Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $c$ and substituting $c=ak$ and $c=bl$, we get the following:*\n> $au + bv|= 1; (au + bv)c|= c; auc + bvc|= c; au(bl) + bv(ak)|= c; ab(ul + vk)|= c$\n> *As $k,l,u$, and $v$ are integers, then $ul + vk = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n> $ab\\cdot m = c$\n> *which implies that*\n> $ab\\: \\mid \\: c$." 13194139534366 17592186045417 false] [17592186199586 1064 "The first property we'll study is called the **transitivity of divisibility.** It states the following:\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ with $a, b\\neq 0,$ we have*\n\n$\\qquad$ $a \\mid b \\quad$ *and* $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c.$\n\nWe can prove this statement using the definition of divisibility.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $a \\mid b,$ there exists an integer $k_1$ such that*\n\n$$\nb=ak_1.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, given that $b \\mid c,$ there exists an integer $k_2$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=bk_2.\n$$\n\n*Substituting the expression for $b$ into our equation for $c,$ we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =b{k}_{2} \\\\  & =(a{k}_{1}){k}_{2} \\\\  & =a({k}_{1}{k}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Since $k_1$ and $k_2$ are integers, their product $k=k_1k_2$ is also an integer. Therefore,* \n\n$$\nc = ak\n$$\n\n*which, by the definition of divisibility, means that $a \\mid c.$*" 13194139534366 17592186045417 true] [17592186199586 1064 "The first property we'll study is called the **transitivity of divisibility.** It states the following:\n\n**Theorem**\n\n> *For integers $a$, $b$, and $c$ with $a, b\\neq 0$, we have*\n> $\\qquad$ $a \\mid b \\quad$ *and* $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c$.\n\nWe can prove this statement using the definition of divisibility.\n\n**Proof**\n\n> *Given that $a \\mid b$, there exists an integer $k_1$ such that*\n> $b=ak_1$.\n> *Similarly, given that $b \\mid c$, there exists an integer $k_2$ such that*\n> $c=bk_2$.\n> *Substituting the expression for $b$ into our equation for $c$, we get*\n> $c|= bk_{2}; = (ak_{1})k_{2}; = a(k_{1}k_{2})$.\n> *Since $k_1$ and $k_2$ are integers, their product $k=k_1k_2$ is also an integer. Therefore,*\n> $c = ak$\n> *which,